---
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro';

const stages = [
  { title: 'Fetch', status: 'Working', body: 'The nightly GitHub Action finds every public repo where your account made commits in the last 30 days, exports each commit with its full diff, scans the export for secrets, and uploads it as an artifact that expires after 10 days.' },
  { title: 'Select', status: 'Working', body: 'Commits already used are skipped, as are denylisted repos and trivial commits such as lockfile-only changes. The rest are ranked by change size, new files, and message quality.' },
  { title: 'Decide the mode', status: 'Working', body: 'If at least one story clears the threshold, shipnote writes a story post. Otherwise it writes one theme post and no tweets.' },
  { title: 'Draft', status: 'Working', body: 'The story goes to the agent you configure, with voice samples and facts from agent-brain. The agent returns structured JSON, and each run records the prompt and config hashes it used. Each call has a time limit, and the agent process is ended with its whole process group when that limit passes or you press Ctrl-C.' },
  { title: 'Validate', status: 'Working', body: 'Every cited SHA must exist in the dataset. Every named file, backticked identifier, or number of two or more digits must appear in the cited commits. Tweets must fit 280 characters. A tweet is dropped when more than 40% of its content words are missing from the commits it cites. A draft that repeats an approved post from the last 30 days is dropped. Claims that fail are logged in review.md.' },
  { title: 'Format for replies', status: 'Working', body: 'Each tweet is written without its link, and the commit links go under "Reply with," since links in the main post reduce reach. Nothing is posted by shipnote.' },
  { title: 'Review and approve', status: 'Working', body: 'You see each draft beside its evidence. Step through the drafts with one key: a approves, r rejects, e edits, and s skips. Nothing is marked used until you run the command that does so, and nothing posts automatically.' },
];
---

<Docs title="How it works: shipnote" description="How shipnote turns your public commits into tweets and blog drafts, with every claim checked against the commit it came from." active="/how-it-works/">
  <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-wider text-sky-400 uppercase">Pipeline</p>
  <h1 class="text-gradient mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.035em] md:text-5xl">How it works</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-7 text-zinc-400">A nightly export runs on GitHub. Drafting and review run on your machine. Each stage either succeeds with evidence or stops and says why.</p>

  <ol class="mt-14 space-y-4">
    {stages.map((s, i) => (
      <li class="gradient-border rounded-2xl p-6">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-sky-400">0{i + 1}</span>
            <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">{s.title}</h2>
          </div>
          <span class:list={['font-mono text-[11px]', s.status === 'Working' ? 'text-sky-400' : 'text-zinc-500']}>{s.status}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7 text-zinc-400">{s.body}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border border-white/[0.08] bg-white/[0.02] p-6">
    <h2 class="font-semibold">When something fails</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-7 text-zinc-400">If the model is down, the dataset is partial, or every draft fails validation, shipnote writes a review file that explains what happened and produces no drafts. It does not retry forever or post a weaker substitute.</p>
  </div>
</Docs>
